##
    Reaching further than a declaration reaches.

    This program does not compile. Four reaches exist, and a declaration that writes no word
    takes the narrowest one that makes sense for it: a member reaches the type that declares it,
    a type reaches the project that declares it. Every word written after that is a widening.

    The last two mistakes are about the words rather than the reach. Two visibilities say two
    things about one declaration, and 'protected' names a line of descent from a declaring type
    — which a type does not have.

    Crossing a project boundary is not shown here, because that takes a second project and this
    folder holds one program per file. samples/library shows it working.
##

model Vault

    integer secret;
    protected integer guarded;
    internal integer shared;
    public integer open;

    function Vault()
        this.secret = 1;
        this.guarded = 2;
        this.shared = 3;
        this.open = 4;
    end function

    integer function Hidden()
        yield this.secret;
    end function

end model

# Two visibilities on one declaration, and 'protected' on a type.
protected model Confused
    public internal integer count;
end model

global model Program

    function Main()
        # The constructor is private, so this type makes its own instances and no one else can.
        Vault v = new Vault();

        # Private: the declaring type and nothing else.
        Console.WriteLine(v.secret);
        Console.WriteLine(v.Hidden());

        # Protected: the declaring type and anything extending it. Program does neither.
        Console.WriteLine(v.guarded);

        # Internal and public both reach here, since this is all one project.
        Console.WriteLine(v.shared);
        Console.WriteLine(v.open);
    end function

end model
